Subject: [PATCH v2 0/13] hv: vmbus: make rings and host-visible buffers
 survive buddy fragmentation

The initial September 17/18 proposal added a plain vzalloc() ring
fallback. Michael Kelley's September 22 review explained why that
breaks Arm CCA and Intel TDX guests without a paravisor: a virtual
mapping cannot be passed to the direct-map page-state operations.
This series instead uses the upstream chunk allocator for rings and
host-visible UIO buffers.

References:
  Initial cover:
    https://lists.openwall.net/linux-kernel/2026/09/18/276
  Review:
    https://www.mail-archive.com/linux-kernel%40vger.kernel.org/msg2658605.html
  Allocator discussion:
    https://lore.kernel.org/all/?q=2026081160447.2529876-1
Base: 93f51579e7df248780214094418f205253383cc5 (Linux 7.3-rc4).

Allocation and lifetime
-----------------------

Rings use vmbus_alloc_buffer() on every allocation, rather than a
second fallback allocator. On ordinary guests the allocator uses
vzalloc(). For host-visible memory on an isolated or independently
encrypted guest, it allocates contiguous chunks, transitions each
chunk through its direct-map address, and then joins them with vmap().
Order descent reaches order zero when larger chunks are unavailable.
The selection predicate includes CC_ATTR_GUEST_MEM_ENCRYPT from the
first patch that introduces the ring consumer.

The buffer address, page and chunk arrays, and embedded GPADL are
owned through struct vmbus_buffer. A separate retained owner survives
release until host ownership, encryption state and mapping references
permit reclamation. Reclaim operates outside the caller and cancels
pending timers before workqueue destruction.

This revision removes the assumption that a host-originated channel
offer rescind revokes every GPADL. Both host rescind and local unload
preserve a possibly delivered GPADL and return -ENODEV from teardown;
neither clears its handle, leak flag or retained host ownership. A
matching GPADL_TORNDOWN acknowledgment can clear ownership. Unknown
creates, unacknowledged teardown and failed page-state transitions
remain retained. A production-entry KUnit case covers established
handles and possibly delivered creates for both rescind origins.

Opaque owner identifiers and lifecycle events record creation, release,
retention, discard and reclamation without addresses. They distinguish
same-sized owners; size-class matching is insufficient. Missing capture
or trace overruns cannot establish complete accounting.

The sysfs ring and /dev/uioN mappings have different contracts. The
sysfs wrapper installs no vm_ops, as required by kernfs, and drops its
bridge pin after mapping setup on every outcome. The UIO path holds its
mapping references through VMA close. The pin snapshots pages, count
and protection state in one owner-lock critical section.

The final tree preserves the existing exported allocator and GPADL
prototypes. Owned entry points serve migrated in-tree callers. The
allocator's argument is temporarily widened in 0001-0008 and restored
in 0009; that intermediate interface change remains explicit.

The requestor array uses kvcalloc(), RNDIS descriptors use
kvzalloc_obj(), and empty netvsc completions handle NULL control
requests without dereferencing them. These guest-private allocations
do not become DMA buffers. Patches 0011-0013 may be routed separately
if the netvsc maintainers prefer; their verified Fixes tags identify
e8b7db38449ac, 5b54dac856cb and 8b31f8c982b7 respectively.

Mapping to the review
----------------------

1. All ring allocations use the existing allocator.
2. Address, page/chunk arrays and GPADL belong to one buffer.
3. The GPADL is embedded. Interior duplication remains because the
   legacy exported establish/teardown interfaces still accept a
   standalone struct vmbus_gpadl. buffer->addr describes the allocation;
   gpadl.buffer describes the GPADL mapping. buffer->size is the rounded
   allocation extent, while gpadl.size is the extent described to the
   host. The retained owner also keeps the allocation size after release.
4. GPADL uncertainty and whole-buffer/page-state retention stay explicit
   through gpadl.leak, buffer.leak and retained-owner state. No generic
   rescind is treated as a teardown acknowledgment.
5. HV_GPADL_BUFFER_DECRYPTED is removed; prepared owned buffers do not
   repeat decryption through the legacy GPADL path.

Validation of this local recut
-------------------------------

The ordered thirteen patches apply to the pinned base and verify against
the SHA-256 manifest. The CoCo static checker passes six invariants and
18 validator self-tests. These are source checks, not platform proof.

Owner accounting passes 22 fixtures, including duplicate/unsafe events,
missing creates and damaged transport. Three replicas require complete
original SHA-256 verification; no kernel event is inferred. The native
pipe reader, three observer and 15 lifecycle-scoring fixtures pass.

The current recut passed 54 linked x86_64 QEMU KUnit cases: 21 VMBus,
14 UIO, 11 netvsc completion, 4 RNDIS and 4 refcount. Two real-skb cases
exercise host success/error, send-slot release, queue 1 statistics and
single synchronous disposal, including duplicate refusal. An actual UIO
unregister case verifies info withdrawal and wakes a registered waiter
while a device reference keeps the notifier alive.

A preceding matched W=1/C=2 comparison reports baseline 4 / candidate 5,
with one repeated inherited flexible-structure diagnostic, no new kind.
The current local configuration emits four inherited diagnostics; it differs
from that comparison. Neither result claims Sparse-clean qualification.

Hosted build 37265334683 passes all three jobs and thirteen sequential
x86_64/arm64 stages, strict cumulative/per-mail style and static checks.
Mainline KUnit passes 54/54; the distinct WSL backport passes 17/17.
Local thirteen-stage application/style and all 21 source files match.
ARM's initial Git shallow-metadata fetch failure precedes application;
the exact-source job retry passes. No source failure is hidden.

Controlled host run 37264609327 completes both candidate lifecycles:
100 cycles, all five subgates, held UIO mappings through removal, kernfs
withdrawal and restored netvsc. Then the host removes the exact disposable
VM adapter; both guests observe target absence and one matching real
offer-rescind event. Both have zero splat/fault and complete verified owner
capture: 684/683 created, 673/672 reclaimed, 9 active and 2 conservatively
retained per guest.
windows-2025 also allocates rings with order-7+ supply exhausted
(499->25->0), without OOM. windows-latest leaves one block (493->26->1):
fragmentation is INCONCLUSIVE/PARTIAL despite a successful lifecycle and
host rescind. Both baseline lifecycles fail; windows-2025 baseline also
OOM-kills the fragmentation shell (exit 137). The workflow's failure
conclusion includes those baseline outcomes. No negative is relabeled PASS.

The controlled experiment first exposed a real panic in hv_uio_rescind
on the preceding candidate after netvsc restoration (37261427641).
A UIO callback survived driver replacement and read the wrong private
data. This recut removes redundant private rescind registration; ordinary
VMBus device unregister already withdraws UIO and wakes readers. The sysfs
helper now starts after UIO readiness, with independent failure scoring.
The corrected real host sequence passes on both runner images.

Earlier runtime 37242875661 lost one owner-transport character and
another candidate plus one baseline timed out in phase 1. Their cause
remains unproven. Later success does not exclude a timing-dependent hang.
The stale KUnit count assertions in 37259972312/37264606869 and preceding
style/Fixes-object failures are corrected, with failed attempts preserved.

Limitations and review questions
---------------------------------

No SEV-SNP, TDX-no-paravisor or Arm CCA page-state transition is
hardware-qualified. The chunked encryption path is not exercised by
an ordinary Hyper-V guest. KUnit and static checks do not replace
those platforms.

The controlled host run exercises one offer-rescind sequence, not every
GPADL response ordering. Please confirm whether an applicable host
protocol guarantee permits GPADL reclamation after offer rescind,
and which in-flight create/teardown orderings it covers. Until then the
code retains uncertain ownership.

UIO removal disconnects after unregister withdraws callbacks. The notifier
stays referenced until channel callbacks stop, while mapping references
survive independently. The matched runtime qualifies ordinary restoration
with live fd and mappings.

Real-skb production-entry KUnit is qualified; hardware traffic and
confidential DMA/page-state failure injection remain unqualified.
The corrected WSL backport is installed locally. Two actual candidate
boots match its selected GNU identity and UIO module/file pair; ten
identity/configuration/log checks pass, with zero critical boot signals.
Baseline binfmt failure and DXG query counts remain disclosed. Actions
37340362101/37340880355 archive the two observations. These are ordinary
WSL backport checks; production admission and CoCo proof remain separate.
The ordinary lifecycle aggregate requires all five subgates, including
restore. PARTIAL and NO_RESULT remain distinct from PASS.

